content.text:
The touch action buttons are STILL displayed on desktop even though `game.isTouch` is false! Why? The CSS rule `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only` only applies with body.touch. But the body.touch class might be added elsewhere? Game constructor adds `.touch` only if isTouch. Console says isTouch false. Maybe `touch-only` class display? `.touch-only { display:none }` should hide. But screenshot shows buttons bottom right. Are those maybe not the touch actions but something else? They are the act buttons with SWAP/DASH/RELOAD/FRAG/FOCUS labels. Yes.

Wait this screenshot is 1024x640 but viewport 1440x900? The screenshot is 1440x900 scaled to 1024x640 by the viewer. The buttons bottom right are displayed. In earlier t1 desktop screenshot, they were NOT present. What changed? My CSS rule `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display: grid; }` — if body doesn't have .touch, irrelevant. But maybe the CSS selector parsing issue: I added it right after `body.touch .touch-only`. Shouldn't affect base.

Hmm, maybe body has class "touch" left from an earlier page/target? Each page is separate. Console `game.isTouch` false but body.className maybe "playing ready touch"? Check: Game constructor adds touch if isTouch. isTouch computed `window.matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches || 'ontouchstart' in window`. On this page with touch emulation disabled explicitly, isTouch false. So no body.touch.

But UI screenshot shows buttons. Could the buttons be shown because `.hud-actions` has `display: grid` and `.touch-only` display none is overridden by `.hud-actions` (specificity .hud-actions (0,1,0) vs .touch-only (0,1,0), later declaration wins). Yes! `.hud-actions` is declared AFTER `.touch-only`? Let's check order: `.touch-only { display:none }` is line ~495, `.hud-actions` is line ~501 → `.hud-actions` display:grid comes later and overrides .touch-only. So the buttons are always visible, even on desktop! In the first desktop screenshot they weren't? Let me recheck the earlier full combat view: I recall no touch buttons at bottom right? Actually maybe they were there but I thought they were part of HUD. In view-combat there were circles at bottom right? Yes! In the 1152 view there were action buttons bottom right. I didn't register them. So the touch controls are always visible on desktop. That's a bug: `display: none` should win.

Fix: make `.touch-only { display: none !important; }` or reorder. Better: `.hud-actions { display: none; }` when not touch? But base says display grid. Use:
```
.touch-only { display: none; }
body.touch .touch-only { display: flex; }
body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display: grid; }
```
Add `body:not(.touch) .touch-only { display: none !important; }` or set `.hud-actions.touch-only { display: none; }` before body.touch. Simplest: change `.touch-only { display: none !important; }` and `body.touch .touch-only { display: flex !important; }`; `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display: grid !important; }`. But avoid !important if possible: move `.touch-only` rule after `.hud-actions` block. There is only one touch-only element (hud-actions), so:
- Remove `.touch-only` rule at 495 and re-add after `.act-btn` styles:
```
.touch-only { display:none; }
body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display:grid; }
```
With source order after .hud-actions, `.touch-only` wins over `.hud-actions`. Then body.touch rule wins.

But media query `@media (max-width:700)` also sets `.hud-tip` etc. no .hud-actions display. There's `body.touch .touch-only { display:flex }` earlier; keep it before. The later `.touch-only` after may override body.touch .touch-only? Specificity body.touch .touch-only (0,2,0) beats .touch-only (0,1,0), so stays flex, then the grid rule beats it. Good.

Let's move the rule. Currently lines:
```
.touch-only { display: none; }
body.touch .touch-only { display: flex; }
body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display: grid; }
body.touch .key-hint { display: none; }
...
.hud-actions { ... display:grid }
```
The `.hud-actions` later overrides `.touch-only`. Solution: change the first rule to `.touch-only { display: none !important; }` and body.touch to `display:flex !important` then grid `display:grid !important` — clunky. Or add at the end of the file a rule `.screen .touch-only { display:none }`? Specificity (0,2,0) equal to body.touch; order last wins for non-touch. But for touch, need display grid.

Best clean approach: remove `display:grid` from `.hud-actions` base (make it `display:grid` only under body.touch), and set base `.hud-actions { display:none }`? But .hud-actions also used for layout? It's only touch controls. So:
```
.hud-actions {
  position: relative;
  display: none;   /* show on touch */
  ...
}
body.touch .hud-actions { display: grid; }
```
And remove `.touch-only` display rules or keep: `.touch-only{display:none}` then `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only{display:grid}` after `.hud-actions`. But `.hud-actions` display:none means no grid layout even on desktop, fine.

Then in body.touch, `body.touch .touch-only { display:flex }` would make it flex before the more specific grid rule; fine. But simplest: keep `.touch-only{display:none}`; set `.hud-actions { display: none; ... }`; add `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display: grid; }` after the `.hud-actions` declaration or at end. The latter rule currently sits BEFORE .hud-actions, so it's overridden by .hud-actions display:none? Specificity body.touch (0,2,0) + .hud-actions.touch-only (0,2,0) = (0,4,0) vs .hud-actions (0,1,0) → high specificity wins regardless of order. So just changing `.hud-actions` display:grid → display:none will hide desktop and body.touch grid wins (0,4,0). Great. But `.touch-only` rule also fine.

Wait then why was `.hud-actions` display:grid overriding body.touch rule on touch previously? Because on touch, `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only` display:grid same value. Before that, `body.touch .touch-only` display:flex (0,2,0) vs `.hud-actions` display:grid (0,1,0): flex should win by specificity regardless of order! But we measured actionsDisplay "flex" before adding the grid rule, confirming specificity; then grid rule fixed it. On desktop, `.touch-only` display:none (0,1,0) vs `.hud-actions` display:grid (0,1,0), later wins → grid. So simply moving `.touch-only` after `.hud-actions` would fix desktop. I'll set `.hud-actions { display:none }` base + `body.touch .hud-actions.touch-only { display:grid }`. This is cleanest.

Also note `.hud-actions` is inside `.hud-bottom`; display none collapses.

Let's edit.

