Wait: this makes it impossible to fire when pointer lock is NOT available (e.g., in unsupported contexts) because a mouse click will always just request lock and return. `document.pointerLockElement` might never become the canvas (e.g., in an iframe without permission). Then the player can't fire with mouse at all. Better: only skip firing if the lock request is likely to succeed. We can attempt lock, and if after the promise rejects, allow firing? Simpler: track `pointerLockFailed` flag. Or just allow firing always (original behavior) — firing on the lock click is a minor issue.

Alternative: fire on mouseup if the lock was just acquired? Overcomplicating. Let's use: if not locked, request lock AND set a pending flag; don't fire on that press. If lock request fails (promise rejection or after 300ms no lock), set `this.mouseFallback = true` and subsequent clicks fire.

Implement:
```
if (e.button === 0 && !this.isTouch && !this.input.mouse.locked && !this.mouseFallback) {
  this.requestLock();
  return;
}
```
And in requestLock, on failure set this.mouseFallback = true; also set a timeout: if after 500ms still not locked, set fallback true. But if the user simply hasn't clicked yet (no lock attempt), fallback shouldn't be set. Set only within requestLock.

requestLock currently catches errors silently. Add:
```
requestLock() {
  if (this.isTouch || document.pointerLockElement === this.canvas) return;
  try {
    const result = this.canvas.requestPointerLock?.();
    if (result && typeof result.catch === 'function') result.catch(() => { this.mouseFallback = true; });
  } catch { this.mouseFallback = true; }
  clearTimeout(this.lockTimer);
  this.lockTimer = setTimeout(() => { if (!this.input.mouse.locked) this.mouseFallback = true; }, 600);
}
```
And reset mouseFallback=false when lock succeeds.

That's reasonable. But note in headless tests, requestPointerLock probably fails? Then the first click is consumed but subsequent ones fire. Our test drives input.mouse.down directly, so unaffected.

Let me implement this.

